4.i 
    We are no longer assuming Rs(T) = Rinf(C)
    tau R' + R = Rinf
    tau = 22

    Linearity:
    By definition of linear ODE's this equation is a first order 
    linear differential equation. Theirfore it has linearity.

    We can also proove it.
    
    Suppose Rinf1 -> R1 and Rinf2 -> R2
    22R'1 + R1 = Rinf1
    22R'2 + R2 = Rinf2

    We need to proove that the combination of outputs corresponds
    to the combination of inputs. 
    Suppose we add both equations after multiplying them by some factor
    a and b respectively

    a22R'1 + aR1 + b22'2 + bR2 = ainf1 + bRinf2

    Re-arranging we get

    22(aR1 + bR2)' + (aR1 + bR2) = ainf1 + bRinf2
    This is the definition of linearity!

    Time-Invarience: 
    Suppose r(t) = rinf(t) - 22r'(t)
    Call the mapping of r(t) to become
    H {z(t), r'(t)} => z(t) - 22r'(t), where r'(t) is fixed as the time 
    derivitve of its output, z(t) is flexible, and the value t is shared.
    H{rinf(t - t0), r'(t - t0)} => rinf(t - t0) - r'(t - t0)

    R(t - t0) = rinf(t - t0) - 22r'(t - t0)

    Definition of time invarience: 
        H{rinf(t - t0), r'(t - t0)} = r(t - t0)

    Theirfore it is time invarient.


